xmodel/ideation-20260831T1033Z-sol56.md:89:The latter is not a slogan: for a unitary representation, Cogolludo Agustín–Florens prove that the twisted Alexander polynomial of a plane-curve complement divides the product of the local twisted polynomials, with the quotient controlled by a Blanchfield form; see *Twisted Alexander polynomials of plane algebraic curves*, J. Lond. Math. Soc. 76 (2007), 105–121, [doi:10.1112/jlms/jdm004](https://doi.org/10.1112/jlms/jdm004), [arXiv:math/0504356](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0504356). A finite permutation representation is unitarizable. Thus a candidate `S4` colouring can be tested against source-cover homology, not merely against a Hurwitz identity tuple. Before promotion one must separately prove the affine/projective-at-infinity convention, torsion hypothesis, and exact linking map; if any is missing the result is `OPEN`, not a vanishing claim.
xmodel/ideation-20260831T1033Z-sol56.md:118:**Evidence.** The quarantined reducible ledger reproduced byte-identically and its documented mutations exited correctly, yet hostile review found hard-coded rows, a tautological `require(3<=3)`, a broken `orbit_size`, and missing cases [W:35–55]. Conversely, same-family pre-review and different-model review caught complementary mathematical failures [W:105–117]. Seal/custody success therefore does not justify changing the paired-review promotion rule; the missing layer is semantic dependency testing.
xmodel/ideation-20260831T1033Z-sol56.md:162:| Paired review and custody | **continue** | Keep the different-model gate and producer-side pre-review where analytic depth warrants it, adding `SEMANTIC-REPLAY/v1`; the two review modes found complementary defects [W:105–117]. |
